# workspace

Traps specific to this crate. Repo-wide rules live in the root `.rules`.

## `Pane` already offers drops to its active item

`Pane::handle_tab_drop` gives the active item first refusal on a dropped tab
before filing it as a sibling tab:

```rust
if is_pane_target && ix == self.active_item_index
    && let Some(active_item) = self.active_item()
    && active_item.handle_drop(self, dragged_tab, window, cx) { return; }
```

An item that manages its own layout — a container holding a nested pane tree —
claims the drop by implementing `Item::handle_drop` and returning `true`.

Why this is worth writing down: the pane renders an absolutely positioned drop
overlay *above* the active item during a drag, so a drop handler on the item's
own element never fires. That symptom points straight at modifying `pane.rs`,
which is both invasive and unnecessary — the seam already exists.

Note `handle_drop` receives `&self` and a bare `App`, with no context to reach
the entity through, so an item that needs to mutate itself must hold a
`WeakEntity<Self>` and defer the work.

## `PaneAxis` keeps three arrays in lockstep

`members`, `flexes`, and `bounding_boxes` are parallel. Any code that mutates
`members` must maintain the other two — `insert_pane` and `remove` are the
existing examples.

`bounding_box_for_pane` opens with `debug_assert!(self.members.len() ==
self.bounding_boxes.lock().len())`, so letting them drift panics in debug
builds as soon as anything reads bounds between a structural change and the
next layout pass. Since layout repairs the arrays on the following frame, the
window where this bites is exactly one frame wide, which makes it easy to
introduce and hard to reproduce.
